# users.txt — EXAMPLE (safe to commit). The IMPORT format for legacy
# credential files (ADR-069).
#
# Credentials live in the app database (`credential` table) — manage them
# with the CLI; never edit hashes by hand (#87):
#
#   cd backend && python -m app.users add <username> <team>
#   cd backend && python -m app.users list
#   (also: remove / set-password / set-team)
#
# This file format survives only as the input to the one-shot migration:
#
#   cd backend && python -m app.users import-file <path-to-users.txt>
#
# A real users.txt holds password hashes: it is git-ignored and must NEVER be
# committed (backend/tests/test_commit_guard.py fails the suite if it is).
#
# Format — one user per line, three colon-separated fields:
#
#   username:password-hash:team
#
#   - username and team are case-insensitive; surrounding spaces are ignored
#   - password-hash is the self-describing PBKDF2 form
#     (`python -m app.password <password>` prints one)
#   - lines starting with '#' and blank lines are ignored
#   - a malformed line (wrong field count, empty field) is skipped, never fatal
#   - a line WITHOUT a team is not a valid credential
#
# Teams: every user belongs to exactly one team; teammates share one lead
# board. The FIRST team listed in the file inherits tenant 1 — the existing
# lead board — so the import preserves line order.
#
# The hashes below are fake placeholders.
alice:pbkdf2_sha256$240000$EXAMPLEsaltEXAMPLEsalt==$EXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEha=:acme-team
bob:pbkdf2_sha256$240000$EXAMPLEsaltEXAMPLEsalt==$EXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEhashEXAMPLEha=:acme-team
